a timely reminder welcome everyone to a  I my name is Jeff eisenach I'm a  visiting scholar here and I'm delighted  to welcome everyone to today's  discussion with the European Union's  commissioner for competition Margaret of  Astaire before introducing Commissioner  of Astaire I'd like to note that we have  probably several luminaries in the room  today but in particular ambassador of  the European Union to the United States  David O'Sullivan is here I wanted to  welcome ambassador O'Sullivan and thank  him for being here on a procedural note  our program will begin today with  remarks from Commissioner of Astaire  after that she's agreed to take some  questions I'll ask the first few and  then we will open it up to the audience  when we do we'll ask that you wait for  the microphone and that you state your  name and your affiliation before asking  your question now it's a great honor for  us to have Commissioner of Astaire with  us today as she's a native of Denmark  and has served in very senior positions  in the in the Danish government  including as Minister of Education from  1998 through 2001 Minister for Economic  Affairs in the interior from 2011 to  2014 she was political leader of the  Danish social Liberal Party and  ambassador of a Sullivan and I were  talking about those words have somewhat  different meanings in Denmark maybe than  they do here but she was political  leader of the Danish social Liberal  Party from 2007 to 2014 and it's my  understanding that she has served as the  role model for the protagonist of a  popular Danish television series about  the election of the country's first  female prime minister so she's not prime  minister but someone plays one based on  her on television in Brussels she served  as president of the economic and  financial Council also known as echo fen  in 2012 and in October 14 in 2014 she  was confirmed by the European  as commissioner for competition since  then she has been responsible for some  of the largest and most significant  antitrust actions in the history of the  European Union well not all of these and  indeed the majority have not involved US  companies certainly the ones that have  gotten the most attention on this side  of the Atlantic have including the abuse  of dominance finding against Google  earlier this year resulting in a fine  the largest antitrust fine in European  Union history of 2.7 billion dollars and  her 2016 finding that Ireland's tax  treatment of Apple has violated the  --use prohibitions against state aid  meaning that Apple owes currently 14  billion dollars in back taxes now  actions like these have won praise from  some and criticism from others here at  AEI we are committed to free enterprise  and competitive markets including  competition in the marketplace of ideas  and with that in mind it's an honor to  welcome Commissioner of astaire to the  American Enterprise Institute we look  forward to your remarks  [Applause]  ladies and gentlemen it is it's a great  pleasure to be here  and I knew I'd feel welcome and it's not  only because of of the very sort of  Danish way my name was pronounced which  is actually quite rare I can tell you  this but also that this institute has a  motto the competition of ideas is  fundamental to a free society and for me  this is what policymaking is all about  if our ideas are never challenged  they'll never get any better we're not  stuck with ideas that once served as  well but haven't kept up with changes in  our world and our societies and when we  don't hear different views well I think  also we lose the freedom to think for  ourselves and just as competition of  ideas is fundamental to a free society  competition in our markets is  fundamental to a fair one because  challenges and competition makes us  better if businesses aren't challenged  if they don't have to compete well they  don't have any reason to work better to  serve people better competition is that  motor that actually drives busines